#!/usr/bin/env node
// bin/pi-run — the fail-closed headless wrapper around `pi` (V-01, FAIL branch).
//
// ============================================================================================
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
// ============================================================================================
//
// V-01 ("does `pi -p` exit non-zero on provider/auth failure?") was run for real on 2026-08-08
// and split. Measured, not assumed:
//
//   pi -p              unresolvable host                     exit 1
//   pi -p              reachable endpoint, wrong key -> 401   exit 1
//   pi -p --mode json  the same 401                           exit 0    <- the defect
//
// The signal is not missing from the stream; only the exit code is. `docs/operations/verification.md`
// (check V-01, FAIL branch) prescribes exactly one remedy — wrap it once, here — and this file is
// that wrapper. It runs `pi` with the caller's arguments and exits non-zero when the run failed,
// whatever `pi` itself returned.
//
// Three measured facts it is written against. Do not "simplify" any of them away:
//
//   1. THERE IS NO `error` EVENT. A failed turn emits, in order: `session`, `agent_start`,
//      `turn_start`, `message_start`, `message_end`, `message_start`, `message_end`, `turn_end`,
//      `agent_end`, `agent_settled` — byte-for-byte the shape of a successful turn. A parser
//      watching for `type == "error"` never fires.
//
//   2. `stopReason` IS NESTED INSIDE `message`, NOT ON THE EVENT. The failing assistant
//      `message_end` carried `message.stopReason == "error"` and `message.errorMessage` with the
//      upstream text, alongside `message.provider` and `message.model`. A parser reading a
//      top-level `stopReason` finds `undefined` on every event and reports success — a silent
//      fail-open, which is the failure mode this wrapper exists to prevent. Note also that the
//      *user* `message_end` in the same stream carries no `stopReason` at all: an absent
//      `stopReason` is normal there and must never be read as a failure. Only the **assistant**
//      message is required to carry one.
//
//   3. `pi -p` WITH STDIN OPEN AND NO TTY BLOCKS FOREVER — no output, no sockets opened, ~0 s CPU.
//      (Print mode merges piped stdin into the prompt, so it waits for EOF that a cron job's
//      inherited stdin never delivers.) This is arguably the most valuable thing the wrapper
//      does: a cron caller that only guards on exit code HANGS rather than fails, and a hang is
//      strictly worse than a failure because nothing alerts on it. `pi-run` therefore always
//      gives the child a stdin of /dev/null (`stdio[0] = "ignore"`, which is documented to make
//      Node open /dev/null and attach it to the child's fd 0) regardless of what its own stdin is.
//      A caller that genuinely wants to pipe a document into `pi` must call `pi` directly and
//      accept that it owns the hang.
//
// ============================================================================================
// WHAT IT DOES
// ============================================================================================
//
// - Forces `--mode json`. If no `--mode` is given, `--mode json` is appended; if `--mode` names
//   anything else, `pi-run` refuses (exit 2) rather than running. JUSTIFICATION: the wrapper's
//   entire value is parsing the event stream, and in any other mode it has nothing to parse. It
//   would then degrade to "exit code only" — which is precisely the fail-open V-01 measured, but
//   now wearing a wrapper's name and inviting callers to trust it. A mode it cannot parse must
//   fail loudly, not quietly become a no-op. (`-p`/`--print` is NOT forced: `--mode json` is
//   already non-interactive, and the caller may have reasons to add `-p` or not.)
// - Parses the stream line by line, and streams the child's own stdout through UNCHANGED, so a
//   caller that wants the JSON still gets every byte in order.
// - Reports failures on stderr in the vocabulary of `extensions/lib/provider-error.ts`'s renderer
//   — same `[pi-config] provider call failed:` summary line, same `label : value` block, same
//   closing `policy` line, same "no truncation" rule for the upstream message. That renderer is
//   deliberately NOT imported: it is TypeScript that pulls in `extensions/lib/once.ts` and the PI
//   package's types, and `bin/` scripts are standalone by contract (see `bin/pi-check`'s header —
//   zero npm dependencies, no import from the repo's TypeScript). Six format strings are the
//   price of a wrapper that runs anywhere `pi` runs. If that renderer's vocabulary changes, change
//   it here too.
//
// ============================================================================================
// EXIT CODES
// ============================================================================================
//
//     0  the stream completed (`agent_settled` seen), at least one assistant `message_end`
//        arrived, and none of them ended the run in `error` or `aborted`.
//     2  a `pi-run` usage error — the wrapper itself could not run. Matches `bin/pi-check`'s
//        "the checker itself could not run" = 2.
//    20  TURN FAILED: an assistant `message_end` carried `message.stopReason === "error"` and was
//        NOT retried away (see THE RETRY DISCRIMINATOR below). This is the V-01 defect; it is the
//        code cron and CI should alert on.
//    21  TRUNCATED STREAM: no terminal `agent_settled` arrived, or no assistant `message_end`
//        arrived at all. A stream that stopped early is a failure, not a success.
//    22  PROTOCOL DRIFT: an assistant `message_end` arrived carrying no `stopReason` field.
//        The stream parsed, but not into the shape this wrapper is written against, so its
//        verdict cannot be trusted — see the "fail-open canary" note at `observeMessageEnd`.
//    23  COMPACTION LOOP: EXT-11's loop guard tripped. V-08 was run for real on 2026-08-08 and
//        FAILED — see THE COMPACTION-LOOP GUARD below — so this is the wrapper-side half of the
//        mechanism the runbook's FAIL branch prescribes.
//    24  TURN ABORTED: an assistant `message_end` carried `message.stopReason === "aborted"`.
//        Its own code, not 20, because the two want different responses: 20 means the model or
//        the provider failed and the credential/endpoint/quota is suspect; 24 means something
//        stopped the turn — a cancelled backoff, a guard, a shutdown — and the provider may be
//        perfectly healthy. PI's own `dist/modes/print-mode.js` exits 1 on both, so leaving 24
//        at 0 would have left `pi -p` and `pi -p --mode json` disagreeing about what a failed
//        run is, which is the whole class of bug V-01 exists to close.
// 25-29  RESERVED for further stream-derived verdicts, same reason as 23.
//   127  `pi` could not be spawned (ENOENT, EACCES). Shell convention for "command not found".
// 128+N  `pi` was killed by signal N. Shell convention. This is also the row a signal FORWARDED by
//        this wrapper lands on — see SIGNAL FORWARDING below.
//     *  any other non-zero code is `pi`'s own, PASSED THROUGH unchanged.
//
// PRECEDENCE. `pi`'s own non-zero exit code always wins over a wrapper verdict — the runbook says
// pass it through rather than replace it, and `pi` knows things the stream does not. The
// diagnostics are still printed in that case; only the number changes. Among the wrapper's own
// verdicts the order is 23 > 20 > 24 > 22 > 21: report the most actionable cause, never let a
// stream that ALSO ended early downgrade a turn that demonstrably failed, and put the loop guard
// first because when it trips, everything downstream of it — including a failed turn — is the
// consequence and not the cause. Every block that applies is printed regardless of which code
// wins.
//
// ============================================================================================
// THE COMPACTION-LOOP GUARD (exit 23)
// ============================================================================================
//
// V-08 asked whether an extension can abort a session from `session_before_compact` in print
// mode. Run on 2026-08-08 against llama-swap, in `-p` and in `-p --mode json`, both answers are
// no, and both are no-ops rather than errors:
//
//   ctx.shutdown()  returned undefined, process still alive. Print mode's `bindExtensions()`
//                   passes no `shutdownHandler`, so `shutdown: () => this._extensionShutdownHandler?.()`
//                   resolves to nothing at all.
//   ctx.abort()     returned undefined, process still alive, `ctx.signal` still undefined and the
//                   compaction `event.signal.aborted` still false. The run then continued into the
//                   turn PI was about to run anyway and exited 0 — byte-identical to the control
//                   run that never called either method.
//
// So the guard cannot end the process from inside the handler through the documented API, and
// `REQ-CTX-35` ("never exit 0 on overflow in headless mode") has to be enforced from out here.
// `extensions/compaction/index.ts` writes a sentinel on every trip, in every mode; this wrapper
// watches for it. Measured, same day, running the shipped extension end to end:
//
//   headlessExitCode: 91  ->  the extension's own `process.exit(91)` DOES work; `pi` exits 91
//                             and the stream stops mid-compaction. 91 is `pi`'s own code and is
//                             passed through — 23 is not needed, and does not fire.
//   headlessExitCode: 0   ->  the guard cancels the pass, the run completes, `agent_settled`
//                             arrives, the failing assistant message carries `stopReason:
//                             "length"` — and `pi -p --mode json` exits **0**. Nothing in the
//                             wrapper's other rules catches this. THIS is what 23 is for.
//
// TWO TRIGGERS, ONE VERDICT. Both carry the same record and either alone is enough:
//
//   1. The sentinel file `<stateRoot>/compaction-loop/<sessionId>.json`, polled every 250 ms once
//      the session id is known. This is the mechanism the runbook's FAIL branch names.
//   2. The `entry_appended` event whose `entry.customType` is `pi-config.compaction-loop`, which
//      `--mode json` was measured to carry. It arrives FIRST — `abortOnLoop` appends the typed
//      entry before it writes the sentinel — and it is the only signal when the sentinel cannot
//      be written at all, which is a real case rather than a hypothetical one: `writeSentinel`
//      is best-effort by design and returns null on an unwritable state root. A sentinel-only
//      wrapper would report success on exactly that run, which is the fail-open this file exists
//      to prevent.
//
// KILLING IS DEFERRED BY 2 s, ON PURPOSE. The trip and the extension's own `process.exit(91)`
// race each other, and a wrapper that killed on sight would return 23 or 91 for the same event
// depending on which won — non-determinism in the one number cron alerts on. So the block is
// printed immediately, and SIGTERM (then SIGKILL five seconds later) is only sent if `pi` is
// still alive after the grace. A run that exits itself keeps its own code; only a run that would
// have carried on gets killed, which is the case the runbook's FAIL branch describes.
//
// STALE SENTINELS. A session id is stable across `--resume`, so a sentinel from an earlier run of
// the same session must not condemn this one. The file counts only when its mtime is at or after
// this process's start (less one second, for filesystems with coarse mtime). The JSON inside is
// read for the report only — a file that exists but does not parse still trips the guard, because
// the decision is "the guard wrote a sentinel for this session", and failing open on a corrupt
// sentinel would be the wrong way round.
//
// The path is recomputed here rather than imported: `stateRoot()` is
// `${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/pi-config` (`extensions/lib/paths.ts:24`) and the id is
// sanitised with `[^A-Za-z0-9._-]` -> `_` (`extensions/compaction/index.ts:261`). `bin/` scripts
// are standalone by contract, the same reason the provider-error renderer is restated above. If
// either of those two lines changes, change it here too.
//
// ============================================================================================
// SIGNAL FORWARDING (SIGINT, SIGTERM, SIGHUP)
// ============================================================================================
//
// A wrapper that dies on SIGTERM and leaves `pi` running is worse than no wrapper: the caller
// gets its exit status back, believes the run is over, and a headless `pi` carries on holding a
// model, a session file and a stdout pipe nobody reads, with nothing left to reap it. launchd and
// systemd both stop a job by signalling the process they started — this one — so that is the
// normal shutdown path, not an edge case. So `pi-run` forwards the signal and then WAITS: the
// child's real fate, not the wrapper's, decides the exit code, exactly as on every other run.
//
//   died of signal N        -> 128+N, the row that already existed. The escalated case reports
//                              SIGKILL (137) because that is what actually killed `pi`; the
//                              stderr line says it was escalated to, and from what.
//   exited with its own code -> that code, passed through, same rule as everywhere else. A `pi`
//                              that traps SIGINT and exits 130 keeps 130.
//   exited 0                 -> the stream's verdict, unchanged — which for an interrupted run is
//                              21 (no `agent_settled`), so an interrupted run still cannot report
//                              success. The wrapper does NOT invent 128+N for a child that did not
//                              die of the signal; inventing one would overwrite the very passthrough
//                              the precedence rule above promises, and would also mislabel the race
//                              where `pi` finished a whole good run microseconds before the signal.
//
// ONLY THREE SIGNALS ARE TRAPPED. SIGKILL and SIGSTOP cannot be trapped at all, so no handler
// pretends to cover them. Nothing else is trapped either: a signal this wrapper has no useful
// answer for must keep its default disposition rather than be swallowed by an empty handler.
//
// REPEATS ARE IGNORED, DELIBERATELY. The second SIGTERM does not re-forward and does not shorten
// the grace. Re-forwarding would be a no-op at best; re-arming the escalation timer would let a
// caller that signals in a loop postpone the SIGKILL forever, which is precisely the hang the
// escalation exists to prevent. The grace is already running and is bounded, and the repeat is
// reported once so the operator knows why nothing new happened.
//
// ONE KILLER, NOT TWO. The compaction-loop guard above also wants to end `pi`, so both go through
// `requestChildExit` and the FIRST caller owns the child — see the comment on that function for
// which one that is in practice and why the loser must not also fire.
//
// ============================================================================================
// THE RETRY DISCRIMINATOR
// ============================================================================================
//
// `config/settings.json` sets `retry.enabled: true, maxRetries: 3`, and PI emits the failed
// attempt's `message_end` into the stream BEFORE retrying. Counting every error `message_end` as
// a failure would make this wrapper exit 20 on runs that succeeded — and a guard that cries wolf
// in cron gets switched off by whoever is on call, which costs more than the defect it catches.
//
// The stream already carries the discriminator. `agent_end` is emitted as
// `{...event, willRetry: this._willRetryAfterAgentEnd(event)}` (`@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent`
// 0.84.0, `dist/core/agent-session.js:366`). So:
//
//   an error `message_end` is NOT a failure  <=>  the NEXT `agent_end` carries `willRetry: true`
//
// and everything else is. Read the second half carefully, because it is where a fail-open would
// hide: an error `message_end` followed by NO `agent_end` at all — a stream truncated immediately
// after the error — stays a failure. Suppression requires positive evidence that PI took over;
// silence is never evidence.
//
// Two refinements, both taken from the source rather than assumed:
//
//   - Only the LAST pending error is suppressed by a `willRetry: true`. `_willRetryAfterAgentEnd`
//     walks `event.messages` backwards and returns on the FIRST assistant message it finds, so
//     the flag speaks for exactly one message. Any earlier error in the same window is promoted
//     to a failure. In practice an agent run stops at its first error so the window holds one,
//     but "in practice" is not a reason to write a rule that fails open when it does not hold.
//   - `aborted` is never suppressible, and this is not a judgement call:
//     `isRetryableAssistantError` returns false unless `stopReason === "error"`
//     (`@earendil-works/pi-ai/dist/utils/retry.js:165`), and `retryAssistantCall` short-circuits
//     an abort before the retry branch with the comment "Abort: terminal but not successful.
//     Never retry an aborted message." So `willRetry` can never be true *for* an aborted message.
//
// A suppressed error is still REPORTED on stderr, as a note rather than a failure block. The
// transient happened; a cron log that hides it entirely is worse than one that explains it.
//
// ============================================================================================
// USAGE
// ============================================================================================
//
//   bin/pi-run -p "say ok" --model databricks/databricks-claude-sonnet-4-5   # --mode json is appended
//   bin/pi-run -p "say ok" --mode json --model local/qwen    # already json: left alone
//   bin/pi-run --help                                        # this table, then exit 0
//
// `PI_RUN_PI_BIN` overrides which binary is executed (default: `pi`, resolved on PATH). Two real
// uses: a cron/launchd job whose PATH does not include `~/bin`, and the fixture-driven tests in
// `test/bin/pi-run.test.ts`, which must never depend on a network, a model or llama-swap.

import { spawn } from "node:child_process";
import { readFileSync, statSync } from "node:fs";
import { constants as osConstants, homedir } from "node:os";
import { join } from "node:path";
import { StringDecoder } from "node:string_decoder";

/** @typedef {"error" | "aborted"} FailingStopReason */
/** @typedef {{ stopReason: FailingStopReason, provider: string, model: string, message: string }} AssistantFailure */
/** @typedef {{ reason: string, events: number }} Truncation */
/** @typedef {{ provider: string, model: string, keys: string }} Drift */
/** @typedef {{ source: string, record: Record<string, unknown> | null }} LoopTrip */

const EXIT_OK = 0;
const EXIT_USAGE = 2;
const EXIT_TURN_FAILED = 20;
const EXIT_TRUNCATED_STREAM = 21;
const EXIT_PROTOCOL_DRIFT = 22;
const EXIT_COMPACTION_LOOP = 23;
// 25-29 stay reserved for further stream verdicts.
const EXIT_TURN_ABORTED = 24;
const EXIT_SPAWN_FAILED = 127;

/** The custom entry `extensions/compaction/index.ts` appends when the loop guard trips. */
const LOOP_ENTRY_TYPE = "pi-config.compaction-loop";
const SENTINEL_POLL_MS = 250;
/** How long `pi` is given to exit on its own after a trip before the wrapper kills it. */
const LOOP_KILL_GRACE_MS = 2000;
/**
 * How long a signalled `pi` is given before SIGKILL — the ONE escalation grace, shared by both
 * things that end a child (the loop guard's SIGTERM and a forwarded SIGINT/SIGTERM/SIGHUP), so
 * that a `pi` cannot be given two different deadlines depending on who asked it to stop.
 *
 * 5 s, chosen from the deadline the wrapper itself is under rather than from taste: launchd sends
 * SIGKILL 20 s after its SIGTERM (`ExitTimeOut`, default 20) and systemd 90 s
 * (`DefaultTimeoutStopSec`). 5 s leaves the wrapper 15 s of the tighter of the two to escalate,
 * reap the child, print its blocks and exit — so the escalation always completes INSIDE the
 * supervisor's window, and the supervisor never has to SIGKILL the wrapper out from under a child
 * it was in the middle of killing, which is the orphan this whole path exists to prevent. It is
 * also comfortably longer than any flush `pi` does on the way out.
 */
const SIGKILL_GRACE_MS = 5000;
/**
 * The signals a caller sends to mean "stop this run": an operator's Ctrl-C, a launchd/systemd
 * stop, a closed terminal. SIGKILL and SIGSTOP are absent because they cannot be trapped, and a
 * handler that listed them would be a lie. Nothing else is listed either — see SIGNAL FORWARDING.
 * @type {NodeJS.Signals[]}
 */
const FORWARDED_SIGNALS = ["SIGINT", "SIGTERM", "SIGHUP"];
/** Slack for filesystems whose mtime resolution is coarser than this process's clock. */
const SENTINEL_MTIME_SLACK_MS = 1000;

/** Width of the label column. `provider-error.ts` uses 9; `stopReason` needs 11. Same shape. */
const LABEL_WIDTH = 11;

const POLICY_LINE =
  "abort — no failover, no substitution, no retry against another provider " +
  "(routing.json onProviderError.policy)";

function usageError(message) {
  process.stderr.write(`pi-run: ${message}\n`);
  process.stderr.write('usage: bin/pi-run <args passed to pi> ("--mode json" is appended if absent)\n');
  process.stderr.write("       bin/pi-run --help    for the exit-code table\n");
  process.exitCode = EXIT_USAGE;
}

function printHelp() {
  process.stdout.write(
    [
      "bin/pi-run — run `pi` headlessly and exit non-zero when the run failed.",
      "",
      "usage: bin/pi-run <args passed to pi verbatim>",
      "",
      "  `--mode json` is appended when absent; any other --mode is refused (exit 2).",
      "  The child's stdin is always /dev/null — `pi -p` with an open stdin and no TTY hangs.",
      "  The child's stdout is streamed through unchanged.",
      "  An error that pi retried away (the next agent_end carries willRetry=true) is reported",
      "  as a note, not as a failure. An error with no following agent_end stays a failure.",
      "  EXT-11's compaction loop guard is watched for on two triggers: the sentinel file",
      "  <stateRoot>/compaction-loop/<sessionId>.json and the pi-config.compaction-loop entry",
      "  on the stream. On either, pi gets 2 s to exit itself and is then SIGTERMed.",
      "  SIGINT, SIGTERM and SIGHUP are forwarded to pi; pi-run then waits for pi to exit and",
      "  reports pi's own fate. A pi that has not exited 5 s after the forward is SIGKILLed.",
      "  Repeat signals are ignored: the escalation is already scheduled and must not be delayed.",
      "",
      "exit codes:",
      "    0  stream completed, no assistant message ended in error or aborted",
      "    2  pi-run usage error",
      "   20  turn failed — an assistant message_end carried stopReason \"error\", not retried away",
      "   21  truncated stream — no agent_settled, or no assistant message_end at all",
      "   22  protocol drift — an assistant message_end carried no stopReason",
      "   23  compaction loop — EXT-11's loop guard tripped and pi still returned 0 (V-08)",
      "   24  turn aborted — an assistant message_end carried stopReason \"aborted\"",
      "  127  `pi` could not be spawned",
      "128+N  `pi` was killed by signal N, including a signal pi-run forwarded to it",
      "    *  any other non-zero code is `pi`'s own, passed through",
      "",
      "  verdict precedence: 23 > 20 > 24 > 22 > 21. Every block that applies is printed.",
      "",
      "env: PI_RUN_PI_BIN   binary to execute (default: `pi`)",
      "     XDG_STATE_HOME  where the compaction sentinel is looked for (default: ~/.local/state)",
      "",
    ].join("\n"),
  );
}

/**
 * Decide the argv handed to `pi`.
 *
 * Scans raw argv tokens, so a *positional* argument whose literal text is `--mode` (a prompt of
 * exactly that) would be misread. That is the accepted cost of not re-implementing PI's own
 * option parser here; every other prompt, including one that merely mentions `--mode`, is safe
 * because it is a single token.
 *
 * @param {string[]} args
 * @returns {{ ok: true, argv: string[] } | { ok: false, given: string }}
 */
function planPiArgs(args) {
  for (let i = 0; i < args.length; i++) {
    const a = args[i];
    if (a === "--mode") {
      const value = args[i + 1];
      if (value === "json") return { ok: true, argv: [...args] };
      return { ok: false, given: value ?? "(no value)" };
    }
    if (a.startsWith("--mode=")) {
      const value = a.slice("--mode=".length);
      if (value === "json") return { ok: true, argv: [...args] };
      return { ok: false, given: value === "" ? "(no value)" : value };
    }
  }
  return { ok: true, argv: [...args, "--mode", "json"] };
}

function field(name, value) {
  return `  ${name.padEnd(LABEL_WIDTH)}: ${value}`;
}

/**
 * The full block for a turn that ended in `error` or `aborted`. Same voice as
 * `formatProviderFailure` in `extensions/lib/provider-error.ts`: summary line, the field block,
 * the closing `policy` line. The upstream text is verbatim and untruncated — rule 2 of that
 * file, and the only thing in the block that carries the actual diagnosis.
 *
 * There is deliberately no `class` line: classification lives in `provider-error.ts`, inside the
 * session, where the diagnostics and cause chain still exist. Out here only the flattened
 * `errorMessage` survives, and guessing a class from it would be a second, weaker vocabulary.
 *
 * @param {AssistantFailure} f
 */
function formatAssistantFailure(f) {
  const aborted = f.stopReason === "aborted";
  const summary = aborted
    ? `[pi-config] pi-run: the turn was aborted: ${f.provider}/${f.model} — stopReason "aborted" ` +
      "(seen by bin/pi-run on the --mode json event stream)"
    : `[pi-config] provider call failed: ${f.provider}/${f.model} — stopReason "error" ` +
      "(seen by bin/pi-run on the --mode json event stream)";
  const policy = aborted
    ? "abort — PI's own print mode exits 1 on an aborted turn while --mode json exits 0; " +
      `bin/pi-run exits ${EXIT_TURN_ABORTED} so the two modes agree on what a failed run is`
    : `${POLICY_LINE}; bin/pi-run exits ${EXIT_TURN_FAILED}`;
  return [
    summary,
    field("provider", f.provider),
    field("model", f.model),
    field("stopReason", f.stopReason),
    field("message", f.message),
    field("policy", policy),
  ].join("\n");
}

/**
 * An error PI itself retried. Not a failure, and not silence either: the transient happened, and
 * a cron log that hides it entirely is worse than one that explains why it was not counted.
 *
 * @param {AssistantFailure} f
 */
function formatSuppressedRetry(f) {
  return [
    `[pi-config] pi-run: note — a provider error was retried by pi: ${f.provider}/${f.model} ` +
      "— NOT counted as a failure",
    field("provider", f.provider),
    field("model", f.model),
    field("stopReason", "error (superseded by the retry)"),
    field("message", f.message),
    field(
      "policy",
      "the agent_end that followed carried willRetry=true, so pi restarted the turn " +
        "(agent-session.js _willRetryAfterAgentEnd); this run's verdict comes from the retry",
    ),
  ].join("\n");
}

/** @param {Truncation} t */
function formatTruncation(t) {
  return [
    "[pi-config] pi-run: the --mode json event stream did not complete — the run is not a success",
    field("reason", t.reason),
    field("events", `${t.events} JSON event(s) parsed before the stream ended`),
    field(
      "policy",
      "abort — a truncated stream is a failure, not a success; " +
        `bin/pi-run exits ${EXIT_TRUNCATED_STREAM}`,
    ),
  ].join("\n");
}

/**
 * `${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/pi-config` — a restatement of `stateRoot()` in
 * `extensions/lib/paths.ts:24`, not an import. See the header on why `bin/` cannot import it.
 */
function stateRoot() {
  return join(process.env.XDG_STATE_HOME ?? join(homedir(), ".local", "state"), "pi-config");
}

/** Mirrors `sentinelPath()` in `extensions/compaction/index.ts:261`, sanitiser included. */
function sentinelPathFor(sessionId) {
  return join(stateRoot(), "compaction-loop", `${sessionId.replace(/[^A-Za-z0-9._-]/g, "_")}.json`);
}

/**
 * The sentinel for this run, or null.
 *
 * Null covers three different things and deliberately does not distinguish them: the file is not
 * there (the normal case, several times a second), it is not readable, or it predates this run.
 * A file that IS there and does bear on this run always returns a trip, even when its contents do
 * not parse — the decision is "the guard wrote a sentinel", and the JSON only decorates the
 * report. Failing open on a corrupt sentinel would be exactly backwards.
 *
 * @param {string} path
 * @param {number} notBeforeMs
 * @returns {LoopTrip | null}
 */
function readSentinel(path, notBeforeMs) {
  let mtimeMs;
  try {
    mtimeMs = statSync(path).mtimeMs;
  } catch {
    return null;
  }
  // A session id survives `--resume`, so a sentinel written by an earlier run of the same session
  // must not condemn this one.
  if (mtimeMs < notBeforeMs - SENTINEL_MTIME_SLACK_MS) return null;

  let record = null;
  try {
    const parsed = JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, "utf8"));
    if (parsed !== null && typeof parsed === "object") record = parsed;
  } catch {
    // Unreadable or half-written. Still a trip; the block below says the contents were unusable.
  }
  return { source: `sentinel ${path}`, record };
}

/**
 * The block for a tripped loop guard. Same voice as the others: summary, field block, closing
 * `policy` line. Every field degrades to a stated absence rather than to `undefined`, because a
 * half-written sentinel is one of the ways this block gets reached.
 *
 * @param {LoopTrip} trip
 * @param {{ killed: boolean, forwarded: NodeJS.Signals | null }} outcome
 */
function formatCompactionLoop(trip, outcome) {
  const r = trip.record ?? {};
  const str = (key, fallback) => (typeof r[key] === "string" && r[key] !== "" ? r[key] : fallback);
  const num = (key) => (typeof r[key] === "number" ? String(r[key]) : "?");
  return [
    "[pi-config] pi-run: the compaction loop guard tripped — the context stopped shrinking and " +
      "the run was not allowed to continue",
    field("session", str("sessionId", "(no sessionId in the record)")),
    field("provider", str("provider", "(no provider in the record)")),
    field("model", str("model", "(no model in the record)")),
    field("trigger", str("reason", "(no reason in the record)")),
    field(
      "passes",
      `${num("automaticPasses")} automatic, ${num("consecutiveNonReducing")} consecutive ` +
        `non-reducing (limit ${num("maxNonReducingPasses")})`,
    ),
    field("why", str("why", str("message", "(the sentinel carried no readable record)"))),
    field("source", trip.source),
    field(
      "action",
      outcome.killed
        ? `pi was still running ${LOOP_KILL_GRACE_MS} ms after the trip and was sent SIGTERM`
        : // Not "pi ended on its own" when a forwarded signal was already ending it — the guard's
          // own kill never fired, but saying nothing took the child would be false.
          outcome.forwarded !== null
          ? `none — the run was already being ended by the forwarded ${outcome.forwarded}, ` +
            "which took the child first"
          : "none — pi ended on its own within the grace period",
    ),
    field(
      "policy",
      "abort — REQ-CTX-35 forbids a headless run returning 0 on an un-shrinkable context, and " +
        `pi -p --mode json returns 0 on one (measured, V-08); bin/pi-run exits ${EXIT_COMPACTION_LOOP}`,
    ),
  ].join("\n");
}

/** @param {Drift} d */
function formatDrift(d) {
  return [
    "[pi-config] pi-run: an assistant message_end carried no stopReason — the event shape this " +
      "wrapper parses has drifted",
    field("provider", d.provider),
    field("model", d.model),
    field("keys", d.keys),
    field(
      "policy",
      "abort — without stopReason the wrapper cannot tell a failed turn from a good one, and " +
        "reporting success would be the exact fail-open V-01 found; " +
        `bin/pi-run exits ${EXIT_PROTOCOL_DRIFT}`,
    ),
  ].join("\n");
}

/**
 * Accumulates the verdict from a JSON event stream, one parsed event at a time.
 *
 * Terminal event: `agent_settled`, and it is chosen over `agent_end` on evidence.
 * `agent_end` is emitted per agent run and carries a `willRetry` flag; with
 * `settings.json`'s `retry.enabled: true` it can fire several times in one prompt, so "saw
 * agent_end" does not mean "nothing more is coming". `agent_settled` is emitted from the
 * `finally` of `_runAgentPrompt` — after `agent.prompt()` AND after the whole
 * `while (await this._handlePostAgentRun()) await this.agent.continue()` retry loop
 * (`@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent@0.84.0`, `dist/core/agent-session.js`) — exactly once per
 * prompt. It is the only event that means the agent is done.
 */
class StreamVerdict {
  /**
   * @param {{ onSessionId?: (id: string) => void, onLoopEntry?: (record: Record<string, unknown>) => void }} [hooks]
   *   Fired while the stream is still open, because both are things the wrapper has to ACT on —
   *   start watching for a sentinel, kill a looping child — rather than only report at the end.
   */
  constructor(hooks = {}) {
    this.hooks = hooks;
    /**
     * Error records waiting to learn whether PI is going to retry them. An entry leaves this
     * queue exactly one of three ways: suppressed by an `agent_end` with `willRetry: true`,
     * promoted to `confirmedErrors` by any other `agent_end`, or promoted by `settle()` because
     * no `agent_end` ever arrived. There is no fourth way out, and in particular no way for an
     * entry to be dropped — that is the fail-open this queue must not create.
     * @type {AssistantFailure[]}
     */
    this.pendingErrors = [];
    /** @type {AssistantFailure[]} */
    this.confirmedErrors = [];
    /** Errors PI retried. Reported as notes, never as failures. @type {AssistantFailure[]} */
    this.suppressedErrors = [];
    /** Aborts are confirmed on sight: PI never retries an aborted message. @type {AssistantFailure[]} */
    this.aborts = [];
    /** @type {Drift[]} */
    this.drifts = [];
    this.sawAgentSettled = false;
    this.sawAssistantMessageEnd = false;
    this.events = 0;
    this.unparsedLines = 0;
    /** The `session` event's `id`. Measured equal to `ctx.sessionManager.getSessionId()`, V-08. */
    this.sessionId = undefined;
  }

  /**
   * @param {string} line one raw stdout line
   */
  observeLine(line) {
    const trimmed = line.trim();
    if (trimmed === "") return;
    let event;
    try {
      event = JSON.parse(trimmed);
    } catch {
      // Not JSON. An extension writing to stdout with console.log lands here, and that must not
      // be fatal: the wrapper judges the events it understands, not the ones it does not.
      this.unparsedLines += 1;
      return;
    }
    if (event === null || typeof event !== "object") {
      this.unparsedLines += 1;
      return;
    }
    this.events += 1;
    if (event.type === "agent_settled") this.sawAgentSettled = true;
    if (event.type === "agent_end") this.observeAgentEnd(event);
    if (event.type === "message_end") this.observeMessageEnd(event);
    if (event.type === "session") this.observeSession(event);
    if (event.type === "entry_appended") this.observeEntryAppended(event);
  }

  /**
   * The stream's first event carries the session id the compaction sentinel is named after —
   * measured identical to `ctx.sessionManager.getSessionId()` inside the extension (V-08). It is
   * the only place the wrapper can learn it without reading PI's config.
   *
   * @param {Record<string, unknown>} event
   */
  observeSession(event) {
    if (this.sessionId !== undefined) return;
    if (typeof event.id !== "string" || event.id === "") return;
    this.sessionId = event.id;
    this.hooks.onSessionId?.(event.id);
  }

  /**
   * EXT-11's typed loop record, on the stream. `abortOnLoop` appends it BEFORE it writes the
   * sentinel, so this fires first, and it is the only trigger left when the sentinel cannot be
   * written at all (`writeSentinel` is best-effort and returns null on failure).
   *
   * @param {Record<string, unknown>} event
   */
  observeEntryAppended(event) {
    const entry = event.entry;
    if (entry === null || typeof entry !== "object") return;
    const e = /** @type {Record<string, unknown>} */ (entry);
    if (e.customType !== LOOP_ENTRY_TYPE) return;
    const data = e.data !== null && typeof e.data === "object" ? e.data : {};
    this.hooks.onLoopEntry?.(/** @type {Record<string, unknown>} */ (data));
  }

  /**
   * Resolve everything waiting in `pendingErrors`.
   *
   * `willRetry` is `_willRetryAfterAgentEnd(event)` (`dist/core/agent-session.js:366`), which
   * walks `event.messages` backwards and returns on the FIRST assistant message it finds. The
   * flag therefore speaks for exactly one message — the most recent — so exactly one pending
   * entry may be suppressed by it. Anything older in the same window is a failure PI is not
   * retrying, and is promoted.
   *
   * `willRetry` is compared with `=== true`, not truthiness: a future PI that dropped the field
   * would leave it `undefined`, and `undefined` must promote, not suppress.
   *
   * @param {Record<string, unknown>} event
   */
  observeAgentEnd(event) {
    if (this.pendingErrors.length === 0) return;
    if (event.willRetry === true) {
      const retried = this.pendingErrors.pop();
      if (retried !== undefined) this.suppressedErrors.push(retried);
    }
    this.confirmedErrors.push(...this.pendingErrors);
    this.pendingErrors = [];
  }

  /**
   * The fail-open canary.
   *
   * Only the ASSISTANT message is judged. The user `message_end` legitimately carries no
   * `stopReason` — measured, V-01 — so a role-blind check would fail every run, and a check that
   * merely tolerated the absence everywhere would report success forever if PI ever renamed the
   * field. Requiring it on the assistant message is what keeps a rename loud instead of silent.
   *
   * @param {Record<string, unknown>} event
   */
  observeMessageEnd(event) {
    const message = event.message;
    if (message === null || typeof message !== "object") return;
    const msg = /** @type {Record<string, unknown>} */ (message);
    if (msg.role !== "assistant") return;
    this.sawAssistantMessageEnd = true;

    const provider = typeof msg.provider === "string" ? msg.provider : "(no provider on the message)";
    const model = typeof msg.model === "string" ? msg.model : "(no model on the message)";

    if (!("stopReason" in msg)) {
      this.drifts.push({ provider, model, keys: Object.keys(msg).sort().join(",") });
      return;
    }
    if (msg.stopReason !== "error" && msg.stopReason !== "aborted") return;

    const record = {
      stopReason: /** @type {FailingStopReason} */ (msg.stopReason),
      provider,
      model,
      message:
        typeof msg.errorMessage === "string" && msg.errorMessage !== ""
          ? msg.errorMessage
          : "(no errorMessage on the message)",
    };
    // An abort is terminal by construction — `retryAssistantCall` returns it before the retry
    // branch and `isRetryableAssistantError` rejects any non-`error` stopReason — so it never
    // waits on an `agent_end` and can never be suppressed by one.
    if (record.stopReason === "aborted") this.aborts.push(record);
    else this.pendingErrors.push(record);
  }

  /**
   * The verdict the stream alone implies, ignoring `pi`'s own exit code.
   *
   * Every applicable block is printed before any code is chosen, so a run that both failed and
   * ended early explains both. The code then follows the documented precedence 20 > 24 > 22 > 21:
   * a failed turn is the answer the caller most needs and the one V-01 exists for, and a stream
   * that also died must never downgrade it — a failed turn whose stream then died is still,
   * first, a failed turn.
   *
   * @returns {{ code: number, blocks: string[] }}
   */
  settle() {
    // Nothing arrived to say PI would retry these, and silence is not evidence of a retry.
    this.confirmedErrors.push(...this.pendingErrors);
    this.pendingErrors = [];

    /** @type {string[]} */
    const blocks = [];
    for (const retried of this.suppressedErrors) blocks.push(formatSuppressedRetry(retried));
    for (const failure of this.confirmedErrors) blocks.push(formatAssistantFailure(failure));
    for (const abort of this.aborts) blocks.push(formatAssistantFailure(abort));
    for (const drift of this.drifts) blocks.push(formatDrift(drift));

    /** @type {Truncation | null} */
    let truncation = null;
    if (!this.sawAgentSettled) {
      truncation = {
        reason:
          "no terminal `agent_settled` event — the stream stopped before the agent was done " +
          "(killed child, crash, closed pipe)",
        events: this.events,
      };
    } else if (!this.sawAssistantMessageEnd) {
      truncation = {
        reason:
          "the agent settled without ever emitting an assistant `message_end` — the run " +
          "produced no assistant message at all",
        events: this.events,
      };
    }
    if (truncation !== null) blocks.push(formatTruncation(truncation));

    if (this.confirmedErrors.length > 0) return { code: EXIT_TURN_FAILED, blocks };
    if (this.aborts.length > 0) return { code: EXIT_TURN_ABORTED, blocks };
    if (this.drifts.length > 0) return { code: EXIT_PROTOCOL_DRIFT, blocks };
    if (truncation !== null) return { code: EXIT_TRUNCATED_STREAM, blocks };
    return { code: EXIT_OK, blocks };
  }
}

/** Shell convention for a child killed by a signal. Unknown names degrade to a plain non-zero. */
function signalExitCode(signal) {
  const number = osConstants.signals[signal];
  return typeof number === "number" ? 128 + number : EXIT_SPAWN_FAILED;
}

function main(argv) {
  const args = argv.slice(2);

  if (args.length === 0) {
    return usageError("nothing to run — pass the arguments you would give `pi`");
  }
  if (args[0] === "--help" || args[0] === "-h") {
    // Handled here rather than forwarded: `pi --help` prints prose, not events, so forwarding it
    // through a stream parser would report a truncated stream and exit 21 on a help request.
    printHelp();
    return;
  }

  const plan = planPiArgs(args);
  if (!plan.ok) {
    return usageError(
      `refusing to run with --mode ${plan.given} — pi-run parses the JSON event stream to decide ` +
        "the exit code, and has nothing to parse in any other mode. Call `pi` directly if you " +
        "want that mode, and own the exit code yourself.",
    );
  }

  const startedAtMs = Date.now();
  const piBin = process.env.PI_RUN_PI_BIN ?? "pi";
  const child = spawn(piBin, plan.argv, {
    // [0] "ignore": Node opens /dev/null and attaches it to the child's fd 0. This is the hang
    //     guard from fact 3 in this file's header, and it is unconditional on purpose.
    // [1] "pipe": needed to parse; every byte is written straight back out, unchanged.
    // [2] "inherit": the child's own stderr is the caller's stderr, untouched.
    stdio: ["ignore", "pipe", "inherit"],
  });

  /** @type {LoopTrip | null} */
  let loopTrip = null;
  let childAlive = true;
  /**
   * Which of the two paths signalled a child that was still running, or null while neither has.
   * Set by `requestChildExit` and never cleared: it is the attribution both the loop block and the
   * `close` handler report from, so it has to outlive the child. One latch and not one flag per
   * path, because two flags are two answers to the single question "who killed pi".
   * @type {"loop" | "forward" | null}
   */
  let endedBy = null;
  /** The signal this wrapper received and passed on, for the report. @type {NodeJS.Signals | null} */
  let forwardedSignal = null;
  /** Whether the grace expired and the child had to be SIGKILLed. */
  let escalated = false;
  /** One repeat note per run: an operator holding Ctrl-C must not paper the log with it. */
  let repeatNoted = false;
  /** @type {NodeJS.Timeout | undefined} */
  let sentinelTimer;
  /** @type {NodeJS.Timeout | undefined} */
  let killTimer;

  const stopSupervising = () => {
    if (sentinelTimer !== undefined) clearInterval(sentinelTimer);
    if (killTimer !== undefined) clearTimeout(killTimer);
    // Once there is no child left to forward to, the handlers must go: a SIGTERM arriving while
    // the wrapper writes its report would otherwise be swallowed by a handler with nothing to do,
    // and a caller that signals a process it can see is still alive is entitled to kill it.
    for (const name of FORWARDED_SIGNALS) process.off(name, onSignal);
  };

  /**
   * THE ONLY PLACE THE CHILD IS EVER SIGNALLED, and the reason there are not two competing
   * killers. Both the compaction-loop guard and a forwarded signal want `pi` to stop; if both
   * fired, the child could take SIGTERM from one path and the other path's SIGKILL a moment
   * later, and no report could then say honestly what killed it.
   *
   * WHOEVER ASKS FIRST OWNS THE CHILD; every later request is dropped, including a request from
   * the other path. Which one that is in practice follows from the clocks rather than from a
   * preference: the loop guard deliberately waits `LOOP_KILL_GRACE_MS` after its trip before it
   * asks (so a `pi` about to `process.exit(91)` keeps its own code), so a signal arriving inside
   * that window forwards first and the guard's kill then never fires — its `action` line says so —
   * while a signal arriving after the guard has already asked changes nothing, because the child
   * is already being killed and re-signalling it would only muddy the attribution. The loop
   * VERDICT is unaffected either way: 23 outranks the stream in the `close` handler exactly as
   * before, and the loop block is printed whichever path took the child.
   *
   * @param {NodeJS.Signals} signal
   * @param {"loop" | "forward"} cause
   * @returns {boolean} true when this call is the one that ended the child
   */
  const requestChildExit = (signal, cause) => {
    if (!childAlive || endedBy !== null) return false;
    endedBy = cause;
    child.kill(signal);
    const hard = setTimeout(() => {
      if (!childAlive) return;
      // The child ignored the signal — SIGKILL cannot be ignored, so this is the bound that keeps
      // the wrapper from waiting on it forever.
      escalated = true;
      process.stderr.write(
        `[pi-config] pi-run: ${piBin} (pid ${child.pid}) did not exit ${SIGKILL_GRACE_MS} ms after ` +
          `${signal} — escalating to SIGKILL\n`,
      );
      child.kill("SIGKILL");
    }, SIGKILL_GRACE_MS);
    hard.unref();
    return true;
  };

  /**
   * Forward, then WAIT — never exit here. Exiting out from under the child is the whole defect
   * this handler fixes: the caller would get a status back while `pi` kept running unwatched and
   * unreapable. The child's own death then produces the exit code through the `close` handler's
   * existing rows (128+N for a death by signal, `pi`'s own code otherwise), so this function
   * decides nothing about the verdict.
   *
   * @param {NodeJS.Signals} signal
   */
  function onSignal(signal) {
    if (requestChildExit(signal, "forward")) {
      forwardedSignal = signal;
      process.stderr.write(
        `[pi-config] pi-run: got ${signal} — forwarded it to ${piBin} (pid ${child.pid}) and is ` +
          `waiting up to ${SIGKILL_GRACE_MS} ms for it to exit; this run's exit code will be ` +
          `${piBin}'s own fate\n`,
      );
      return;
    }
    if (repeatNoted) return;
    repeatNoted = true;
    process.stderr.write(
      childAlive
        ? `[pi-config] pi-run: got ${signal} while ${piBin} is already being shut down — ignored. ` +
          `The SIGKILL escalation is already scheduled and re-arming it on every signal would let ` +
          "a caller that signals in a loop postpone it forever\n"
        : `[pi-config] pi-run: got ${signal} after ${piBin} had already exited — ignored; ` +
          "pi-run is finishing its report\n",
    );
  }

  for (const name of FORWARDED_SIGNALS) process.on(name, onSignal);

  /**
   * One trip per run: the sentinel and the stream entry carry the same record, and whichever
   * arrives first owns the report. The kill is deferred so that a `pi` whose own extension is
   * about to `process.exit(91)` keeps its own exit code — see the header.
   *
   * @param {LoopTrip} trip
   */
  const onLoopTrip = (trip) => {
    if (loopTrip !== null) return;
    loopTrip = trip;
    if (sentinelTimer !== undefined) clearInterval(sentinelTimer);
    killTimer = setTimeout(() => {
      requestChildExit("SIGTERM", "loop");
    }, LOOP_KILL_GRACE_MS);
    killTimer.unref();
  };

  const verdict = new StreamVerdict({
    onSessionId: (id) => {
      const path = sentinelPathFor(id);
      sentinelTimer = setInterval(() => {
        const trip = readSentinel(path, startedAtMs);
        if (trip !== null) onLoopTrip(trip);
      }, SENTINEL_POLL_MS);
      // The child keeps the event loop alive; this timer must never keep it alive by itself.
      sentinelTimer.unref();
    },
    onLoopEntry: (record) => {
      onLoopTrip({ source: `the ${LOOP_ENTRY_TYPE} entry on the --mode json event stream`, record });
    },
  });
  const decoder = new StringDecoder("utf8");
  let pending = "";
  let spawnFailed = false;

  child.stdout.on("data", (chunk) => {
    process.stdout.write(chunk);
    pending += decoder.write(chunk);
    let newline = pending.indexOf("\n");
    while (newline !== -1) {
      verdict.observeLine(pending.slice(0, newline));
      pending = pending.slice(newline + 1);
      newline = pending.indexOf("\n");
    }
  });

  child.on("error", (err) => {
    spawnFailed = true;
    childAlive = false;
    stopSupervising();
    process.stderr.write(
      `[pi-config] pi-run: could not run "${piBin}" — ${err.code ?? "error"}: ${err.message}\n` +
        "  set PI_RUN_PI_BIN to an absolute path, or put pi on PATH\n",
    );
    process.exitCode = EXIT_SPAWN_FAILED;
  });

  child.on("close", (code, signal) => {
    childAlive = false;
    stopSupervising();
    // A spawn failure has already decided the exit code; `close` still fires after `error`, and
    // judging an empty stream on top of it would replace 127 with a less informative 21.
    if (spawnFailed) return;
    pending += decoder.end();
    verdict.observeLine(pending);
    // Last look: a `pi` that exited the moment it tripped can beat the 250 ms poll to the finish.
    if (loopTrip === null && verdict.sessionId !== undefined) {
      const late = readSentinel(sentinelPathFor(verdict.sessionId), startedAtMs);
      if (late !== null) loopTrip = late;
    }

    const settled = verdict.settle();
    for (const block of settled.blocks) process.stderr.write(`${block}\n`);
    if (loopTrip !== null) {
      process.stderr.write(
        `${formatCompactionLoop(loopTrip, {
          killed: endedBy === "loop",
          forwarded: forwardedSignal,
        })}\n`,
      );
    }

    if (signal !== null && signal !== undefined) {
      // Our own SIGTERM is not `pi` dying: reporting 128+15 would blame the child for the
      // wrapper's decision and hide the reason behind a signal number.
      if (endedBy === "loop") {
        process.exitCode = EXIT_COMPACTION_LOOP;
        return;
      }
      // A forwarded signal is the opposite case: the caller asked for exactly this death, so
      // 128+N is the answer it is owed rather than a wrapper verdict. The signal reported is the
      // one that actually killed `pi` — SIGKILL when the escalation had to fire — because that is
      // the child's real fate; the line below says what it was escalated from.
      process.stderr.write(
        forwardedSignal !== null
          ? escalated
            ? `[pi-config] pi-run: ${piBin} ignored the forwarded ${forwardedSignal} for ` +
              `${SIGKILL_GRACE_MS} ms and was killed by ${signal}\n`
            : `[pi-config] pi-run: ${piBin} exited on the forwarded ${forwardedSignal} ` +
              `(killed by ${signal})\n`
          : `[pi-config] pi-run: ${piBin} was killed by ${signal}\n`,
      );
      process.exitCode = signalExitCode(signal);
      return;
    }
    if (forwardedSignal !== null) {
      // The child handled the signal and chose its own code, so it did not die of the signal and
      // 128+N would be a fiction. Its code — or, when that code is 0, the stream's verdict, which
      // for an interrupted run is 21 because no `agent_settled` arrived — decides below, exactly
      // as on an unsignalled run. Said out loud because a reader who sees 21 after a SIGTERM must
      // be able to tell that from the wrapper having lost track of the signal.
      process.stderr.write(
        `[pi-config] pi-run: ${piBin} handled the forwarded ${forwardedSignal} and exited with ` +
          `code ${code} of its own — that code, or the stream's verdict when it is 0, decides ` +
          "this run; pi-run does not invent 128+N for a child that did not die of the signal\n",
      );
    }
    // `pi`'s own non-zero code is passed through, never replaced — it may encode something the
    // event stream does not carry. In particular EXT-11's own `headlessExitCode` (91 by default)
    // arrives here, and it is a better answer than 23 because the extension chose it. The blocks
    // were printed above regardless.
    if (typeof code === "number" && code !== 0) {
      process.exitCode = code;
      return;
    }
    // A tripped guard outranks every stream verdict: whatever else the stream showed happened
    // downstream of a context that had stopped shrinking.
    if (loopTrip !== null) {
      process.exitCode = EXIT_COMPACTION_LOOP;
      return;
    }
    process.exitCode = settled.code;
  });
}

main(process.argv);
